    {
      "blockId": "tour:itinerary_text:4b5ff9b4a37f43cc",
      "field": "itinerary.text",
      "sourceTextSha256": "474a809af20f277454d1643e4508f92ee2d4736e51e44c29d3ebc37310e5ed33",
      "text": "After breakfast, continue the jeep tour.\n\nThe route passes through the small lagoons of Honda, Hedionda and Cañapa, as well as other high-altitude lakes.\n\nYou will stop at a viewpoint overlooking Ollagüe Volcano.\n\nThe volcano is active, with a plume continually visible above its summit.\n\nThe route then crosses the Chiguana salt flats, which contain deposits of sodium and potassium nitrate.\n\nThe main stop of the day is Salar de Uyuni.\n\nThe salt flat covers around 11,000 km² and lies at an altitude of about 3,650 m. In its central section, the salt crust reaches a thickness of 10 m.\n\nIn the dry season, the surface becomes a vast white plain. In the rainy season, a thin layer of water mirrors the sky.\n\nThis reflection makes for photographs with unusual perspectives, especially across the even surface of the salt flat.\n\nIn Colchani, you will learn how salt is extracted and processed.\n\nYou will also stop at the natural feature known as the “Eyes of Water”.\n\nThe route then continues to Incahuasi Island, with giant cacti and panoramic views of the salt flat.\n\nA packed lunch is included.\n\nOvernight at a hotel built from salt.",
      "provenance": {
        "kind": "ai_translation",
        "runId": "ada-en-mw6krd1q-6gkvrjmldy",
        "model": "GPT-6"
      }
    },
